        "answer": {
          "text": "When the Paws process starts, it invokes `recoverOnStartup` to assess the state of all work items that were \"in flight\" (specifically those in `awaiting_input`, `running`, `verifying`, `recovering`, or `merge_queue` states) (`src/db/recovery.ts:53`, `src/db/recovery.ts:31`). Paws is designed to be safe to restart while work is ongoing, as remote runners continue to operate during downtime and preserve their results until the process returns (`README.md:54`).\n\nFor each in-flight item, Paws uses `decideStartup` to determine the appropriate recovery action (`src/db/recovery.ts:66`, `src/core/recovery.ts:75-83`). The behavior varies by state and context:\n\n1. **Merge Queue**: Items waiting in the merge queue keep their position (`src/core/recovery.ts:95`). If there is evidence that a merge call may have been in flight, the system checks GitHub to verify if the call succeeded (`src/core/recovery.ts:96`).\n2. **Verifying**: If an item is in the `verifying` state and a runner still holds its Closeout review or check run, Paws re-verifies the work and attaches to that existing runner session (`src/core/recovery.ts:81-82`, `src/core/recovery.ts:98`).\n3. **Running/Recovering**: These items generally resume in a fresh session using their last checkpoint (`src/core/recovery.ts:100`, `src/core/recovery.ts:105`).\n4. **Crash Protection**: If an item has already been resumed once and a short amount of time has passed (less than `RESUME_AGAIN_AFTER_MS`), Paws pauses to ask the user in Linear whether to start the work again, preventing potential crash loops (`src/core/recovery.ts:101-102`, `src/core/recovery.test.ts:82-90`).\n\nIf the decision is to resume, the system attaches to the existing runner work where applicable, ensuring that ongoing Closeout reviews and answer tasks are not lost (`README.md:54`, `src/core/recovery.ts:100`). When multiple items are recovered, Paws staggers the resume calls to avoid overloading the system (`src/db/recovery.ts:56`).\n\nThis is based on 17 source ranges in 15 files, and research stopped at the deep limit maxPasses, so other relevant code may not have been inspected.",
